{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1729 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "8a6c12279c1aea259f7415c960bc625f743ffe6ed6e3527eb92f11649820b350" :edb/db-before-t 1728 :edb/db-after-t 1729 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535041 50 #inst "2026-10-08T15:29:27.632Z" 13194139535041 true] [17592186128920 1145 "The function $f(x,y)=e^{\\sqrt{xy}}$ is continuous on $D=[0,2]\\times[0,1]$. In II and III, each integration interval belongs to the variable being integrated; Fubini's theorem makes both expressions equal to $\\iint_D f\\,dA$.\n\nIn I, the integral is over the reflected rectangle $D^*=[0,1]\\times[0,2]$. Set $u=y$ and $v=x$. The absolute Jacobian is $1$ and $f(v,u)=f(u,v)$, so\n\n$$\\int_0^1\\int_0^2 e^{\\sqrt{xy}}\\,dy\\,dx\n=\\int_0^1\\int_0^2 e^{\\sqrt{uv}}\\,du\\,dv\n=\\iint_D e^{\\sqrt{xy}}\\,dA.$$\n\nAll three displayed equalities are therefore true. The source's \"II and III only\" answer correctly recognizes the two integrals with bounds directly assigned to the stated coordinates, but overlooks the equality of I caused by symmetry." 13194139535041 true] [17592186128920 1145 "We notice that: \n\n- the limits for the variable $x$ are from ${\\color{blue}0}$ to ${\\color{blue}2}$, and\n\n- the limits for the variable $y$ are from ${\\color{red}0}$ to ${\\color{red}1}.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the region of integration is rectangular, we can simply swap the order of integration. This gives\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} e^{\\sqrt{xy}} \\,\\text{d}A  \n= \\int_{\\color{blue}0}^{\\color{blue}{2}} \\left[ \\int_{\\color{red}0}^{\\color{red}1} e^{\\sqrt{xy}}\\,\\text{d}{\\color{red}y} \\right] \\text{d}{\\color{blue}x} .\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." 13194139535041 false]] :edb/tempids {}}
